One INFO line after "GPU KV cache size": how many requests the pool holds resident at ~1k
tokens, 32k and max_model_len, from the engine's own KV cache groups.

max_num_seqs caps admission, not residency. On this hybrid model every resident request holds
recurrent-state blocks for its whole life (6 GDN groups x (2 + k) blocks in align mode, 1 + k
without prefix caching), so the pool holds far fewer requests than max_num_seqs admits: on the
shipped bf16 CTX=fast pin, 6 at ~1k-token prompts and 1 at 61k, against max_num_seqs=8. The
rest wait, and a full pool preempts and recomputes. The existing line gives one number,
"Maximum concurrency for <max_model_len> tokens", which says nothing about short prompts.

The figures come from each device group's max_memory_usage_bytes, the admission cap that pool
sizing and the runtime admission gate share, evaluated at each length instead of
max_model_len, and from num_blocks - 1 (block 0 is the null block). No model shapes are
assumed, so the line holds for any model, KV dtype, group layout and mamba cache mode. At the
shortest length it prints a range: the low end charges the full cap, the high end only the
blocks that do not grow with context. Measured residency at ~1k sits between the two because
the engine charges a short request less context than the cap allows (none at all on int8 KV).

Checked against four boots on the reference RTX 3090 (vLLM 0.31.0, batch 5 Q2: bf16 k=7, int8
k=7 at two pool sizes, int8 k=3; held waves of identical requests, peak Running):
bench/test_kv_cache_seat_budget.py replays each with vLLM's own spec classes. The cost per
request at max_model_len equals what each engine reported (num_gpu_blocks /
kv_cache_max_concurrency), resident counts at ~1k fall inside the range, and the 32k and
max_model_len figures equal what the boot held at 32.8k and 61.5k.

The single-user launcher prints its own prediction before boot (single-user/start_qwen.sh);
this line is the engine's word on the same pool. Upstream vllm #54840 (open) logs a per-group
block budget at the same point; this patch retires when that, or an equivalent, lands.

